<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Hand-trimmed fixture standing in for WindowsDefender-All-2.8, the latest revision PowerStig
  ships of the Defender Antivirus STIG (#110's map). Rule ids, field values and dscresourcemodule
  attributes are copied from the real processed data in the local PowerStig clone rather than
  invented, trimmed the way the Google-Chrome and MS-Edge fixtures are (short Description, no
  VulnDiscussion/RawString prose, no LegacyId).

  Defender carries one rule type: Registry (all 67 rules, every one the RegistryPolicyFile
  dscresource, every one HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE). No Manual, no Document, no unparsed rule and no
  .a/.b sub-rule pair, so there is nothing here to assert about those.

  The seven rules kept are the shapes #110's survey called out. V-213426 is the ordinary
  Dword/Present rule (44 of 67) and V-213456 the String/Present one (20 of 67) - the latter an
  ASR rule, whose ValueName is a GUID and whose Key carries spaces, neither of which any prior
  product's registry fixture has had. V-213428/29/30 are the three Ensure=Absent rules, each also
  carrying ValueType None and an empty ValueData, which ADR 0014 settled. V-213450 and V-213455
  are two of the seven organisation values, both answered by the real org.default.xml; V-213455's
  ValueName is the bare digit 5, which Chrome's V-221563 already proved reaches
  Get-AnsibleVariableName intact, and it is carried here because Defender has four of them.
-->
<DISASTIG version="2" classification="UNCLASSIFIED" customname="" stigid="MS_Defender_Antivirus" description="Fixture STIG." filename="U_MS_Windows_Defender_Antivirus_STIG_V2R8_Manual-xccdf.xml" releaseinfo="Release: 8 Benchmark Date: 01 Apr 2026 3.5.2 1.10.0" title="Microsoft Defender Antivirus Security Technical Implementation Guide" notice="terms-of-use" source="STIG.DOD.MIL" fullversion="2.8" created="6/16/2026">
